@page "/"

<h1>Collaboration, in C#, with no server in the middle</h1>

<p class="lede">
    bit Bcrdt is a native .NET implementation of a conflict-free replicated data type - a
    CRDT - together with the synchronization, presence, persistence and Blazor integration that a
    collaborative feature actually needs. There is no JavaScript CRDT library underneath it and no
    port of one: the engine, the wire format and the merge algorithm are all C#.
</p>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>What a CRDT gives you</h2>
    <p>
        Suppose two people edit the same paragraph at the same moment and neither can see the other's
        change yet. Without a CRDT you have to pick a loser. The usual approaches are to lock the
        document so only one person can type, to funnel every keystroke through a server that
        rewrites everyone's edits into a single agreed order, or to save last and overwrite the other
        person's work. All three either need a permanently reachable authority or silently destroy
        something somebody wrote.
    </p>
    <p>
        A CRDT removes the need to choose. Every edit is expressed so that applying edits in any
        order, more than once, or after an arbitrarily long delay always produces the same result on
        every copy. That single property has consequences that stack up quickly: no server is
        required to arbitrate, replicas can be edited while completely disconnected and reconciled
        later, updates are small deltas rather than whole documents, and nobody's work is thrown
        away. What you give up is the ability to say <em>which</em> of two truly simultaneous writes
        to the same single value wins - the answer is deterministic and identical everywhere, but it
        is arbitrary. Where losing a write is unacceptable, you model the value as a nested
        collaborative type instead of a scalar, and both sides survive.
    </p>

    <h2>What the smallest useful program looks like</h2>
    <p>
        Opening a document does everything in the right order - load from storage so the document is
        usable immediately and offline, then connect and let the handshake reconcile whatever changed
        while it was away. After that, editing is ordinary method calls.
    </p>

    <pre class="code"><code>// Program.cs - one registration configures transport and storage.
builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtServices(collaboration => collaboration
    .UseTransport(documentId =&gt; BcrdtInMemoryNetwork.Shared.CreateTransport(documentId))
    .UseInMemoryStore());

// Anywhere with the service injected.
BcrdtCollaborativeDocument document = await collaboration.OpenAsync("my-document");

document.Text("body").Insert(0, "Hello, everyone.");
document.Map.Set("title", "Draft");
document.Presence.SetUser(new BcrdtCollaboratorInfo("ada", "Ada", BcrdtPresenceService.SuggestColor("ada")));

// That is the whole thing. The edits are already merged locally, already encoded as a delta,
// already on their way to every other replica, and already written to storage.</code></pre>

    <h2>The demos</h2>
    <p>
        Each page below - except the live one - shows several replicas of one document, side by side, in this single browser
        page. They are not views onto a shared object - each panel is a genuinely separate replica
        with its own client identifier, its own presence identity, its own local storage and its own
        connection, talking to the others through the library's in-memory transport. That is what
        makes the merges you see real merges rather than a shared variable being redrawn.
    </p>

    <dl class="demo-list">
        <dt><a href="text">Collaborative text</a></dt>
        <dd>
            Three replicas editing one paragraph. Type in any of them and watch it appear in the
            others. Take one offline, edit it and the others, bring it back, and watch a genuine
            divergence be reconciled without anybody losing a word. This is the headline demo.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="list">Collaborative list</a></dt>
        <dd>
            A shared to-do list: a sequence whose elements are themselves collaborative maps. Shows
            what nesting buys you - editing an item's title merges with a concurrent tick of its
            checkbox, because they are different keys of a real map rather than two writes of one
            blob.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="form">Collaborative form</a></dt>
        <dd>
            Several fields on a map, plus one long-text field, so the difference in merge behaviour
            between a scalar and a text type is visible next to each other in the same form.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="conflicts">Conflict lab</a></dt>
        <dd>
            The page that proves the claims. Pause each replica's sync, make specific conflicting
            edits on purpose - concurrent insert at the same position, concurrent delete of the same
            element, concurrent write of the same key - then resume and compare the raw state of both
            replicas as JSON.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="undo">Collaborative undo</a></dt>
        <dd>
            Undo that reverses your own last action and leaves everyone else's work alone, which is
            what users expect and what a naïve "restore the previous state" undo gets badly wrong in
            a shared document.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="internals">Internals</a></dt>
        <dd>
            An inspector: live state vector, block count, encoded size and a log of updates as they
            are produced. Type five hundred characters and watch the block count stay in single
            figures - that is the run-length encoding that makes large documents affordable.
        </dd>

        <dt><a href="live">Live, across tabs</a></dt>
        <dd>
            The same text editor, one replica per browser tab, meeting through the demo server's
            SignalR hub or WebSocket endpoint - which relay messages and hold no copy of the document.
            Share the link with another tab or device and type in both.
        </dd>
    </dl>
</section>
